From bde140ba415bd363e6a22aababe5d6a7b4d84aed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 19:53:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Gradients and inner forces computed by kernels
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

First kernels of the batch path (lot L3), in `src/Batch/`, compiled as
CUDA code when MFEM is compiled with CUDA:

- gradients: element restriction (native ordering) and physical
  derivatives at the integration points by the quadrature interpolator of
  MFEM, once per integration rule for all the materials
  (`batch::computeUnknownsDerivatives`), then one kernel per material
  writing the gradients of the behaviour (linearised strain or deformation
  gradient, macroscopic gradients included) in the array of the material,
  in its layout (`batch::computeGradients`);
- inner forces: gradients of the shape functions on the reference element
  (`DofToQuad`), jacobians and determinants (`GeometricFactors`), one
  thread per node of an element, `Bᵀ σ w det J` with the operator `B` of
  the generated behaviour integrators, added to values element by element
  in the layout of the element restriction (`batch::addInnerForces`); the
  orthotropic behaviours use the thermodynamic forces in the global frame
  of all the points;
- behaviour integrators: `supportsKernels`, `computeGradientsByKernels`,
  `addInnerForcesByKernels`, implemented by the standard CRTP base
  (mechanics in 3D, plane strain and plane stress, small and finite
  strain, without FBar, one integration rule for all the elements of a
  material); `PartialQuadratureSpace::getUniformIntegrationRule`,
  `Material::getMacroscopicGradients`;
- new parameters of the non linear evolution problems, defaults given by
  environment variables: `GradientsComputation` (`ElementByElement` or
  `Kernels`, with `BehaviourIntegration` set to `Batch`) and
  `InnerForcesComputation` (`ElementByElement` or `Kernels`: the integrator
  of mfem-mgis gives null element vectors to MFEM, and `Mult` adds the
  inner forces computed by kernels, summed by the transposed element
  restriction and prolongation); refused with PETSc.

`QuadratureInterpolator::MultTranspose` is not implemented in MFEM 4.10:
the kernel of the inner forces is written here.

Tests: GradientsKernelsTest compares the gradients and the inner forces
computed by kernels to the ones computed element by element (hexahedra and
tetrahedra, orders 1 and 2, small and finite strain, orthotropic, plane
strain, two materials with macroscopic gradients; differences of the order
of 1e-16), on the host, on the debug device of MFEM and on the GPU;
BatchIntegrationTest compares complete computations with the kernels to
the integration point by point (differences below 1e-12, the numbers of
Newton iterations differing at most by one at each time step). 142/142;
with all the kernels enabled by the environment variables, only the
expected refusals fail (heat transfer, micromorphic damage).
